00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
the u.n says registering refugees is  critical to ensuring they get assistance  and protection  800 000 rohingya refugees in bangladesh  gave personal information including  fingerprints to the unhcr beginning in  2017.  now human rights watch says that  information was improperly handled and  shared with myanmar  when they had their data taken by unhcr  this was part of a more general  registration process by the un agency  to give them access to the most basic  and essential services once they had  arrived in  in bangladesh and um what the government  in bangladesh issued for each of them  which is the smart card with some  biometric uh  information attached to it and in the  context of that unhcr decided to  also register these individuals um  for their data to be sent to myanmar now  what we found in our interviews  is that the people we spoke to all  except one had never even been  asked by by unhcr staff when being  registered in the field  whether they agreed to having their data  shared with myanmar  the unhcr has strongly denied any  wrongdoing  it insists consent was obtained from the  refugees at least twice  adding that when unhcr and the  government of bangladesh signed their  memorandum of understanding on data  sharing in january 2018  and organized their joint registration  exercise of rohingya refugees  specific measures were taken to mitigate  potential risks  al-jazeera has reached out to the  governments of bangladesh and myanmar  for comment  but received no reply in 2017 myanmar's  military launched a violent crackdown  against the rohingya  in rakhine state the u.n called it a  textbook example of ethnic cleansing  more than 700 000 rohingya escaped  across the border to southwestern  bangladesh  according to human rights watch during  the refugee registration process  some rohingya even raised concerns their  data would be used to send them back to  myanmar  the government of bangladesh has  repeatedly stated that repatriations  would only be carried out on a voluntary  basis  unhcr says it will assist refugee  returns if they are voluntary  and when conditions are favorable for a  safe and sustainable return  which is not the case at the moment but  for the rohingya  considered one of the world's most  persecuted minorities the worries are  constant  and they're not likely to ease in the  wake of a new report calling into  question  who may have had access to their  personal information  mohammed al jazeera
 

